import "core/maybe.ct"
import "core/string.ct"
import "core/webdoc.ct"


module(Data/Calendar)
  # # Calendars
    A calendar lays out the things a page reaches which sit on a day, on the days they sit on.
    What such a thing is, is not decided here. This declares only what a calendar needs to know
    about one — which day it is assigned to — and leaves every answer to whoever has something to
    show. So a calendar shows a kind of thing this file has never heard of, and adding one is
    saying that it belongs and answering that question, both written where that kind of thing is
    declared.
  class(CalendarEntry)
    # [CalendarEntry] is the class of things a calendar can show. A task is one, which
      @stack/core/note.ct@ says where a task is declared rather than this file saying it here.
      Membership is declared by the member and not by the class for the reason the class exists: a
      calendar which had to be told about each of its members would be a calendar of a fixed list of
      things, and the list would live in the one file that has no reason to know what is on it.
      There is a second reason it must be that way round, which is a limitation rather than a
      choice. An arm answering an abstract method for a type that has fields is not read as
      fulfilling the declaration unless it is written in a different file from the declaration. So
      [calendarDay] is declared here and answered elsewhere, and this file sits below the files
      holding its members rather than above them. The layering that forces is the one wanted anyway.

  every(/Data/Maybe[$T -> /Catln/Doc/DShow], isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])
    # A [/Data/Maybe] of something showable is showable, which is what lets an answer of
      [calendarDay] be evaluated at all: a tree is built toward a class, and a tree built toward a
      value's own type comes back as the unevaluated application of it.
      Said here rather than in @stack/core/maybe.ct@, where it would read as the more general truth
      it is. That file does not reach the webdocs, and having it reach them would put it in the
      SCC that @compile.ct@ and @webdoc.ct@ are in, which is a cost paid by every compile for the
      sake of one caller. It moves there when there is a second one.

  :$E.calendarDay[$E -> /Data/Calendar/CalendarEntry] -> /Data/Maybe[$T -> String]
    # [calendarDay] is the day an entry is assigned to, as the @YYYY-MM-DD@ string it is written as.
      A day is a string here as it is everywhere a note is read: Catln has no date, and inventing
      one for a calendar would be inventing it for the whole language rather than for a calendar.
      An entry which sits on no day answers [/Data/Maybe/Nothing]. That is an answer and not a failure to
      give one — an undated entry is shown apart from the days rather than dropped, because a
      calendar quietly showing less than it was handed would be answering a narrower question than
      the one asked.
      The receiver is bound as [$E] and not as [$T] because [/Data/Maybe] names its own parameter
      [$T] and a parameter resolves by name, so a receiver of that name would be the same one.

  annot(#calendar, isa= [/Catln/Doc/DocPage])
    # A calendar of everything the page it is written in reaches which sits on a day.
      It takes nothing. What to show is what is there: every [CalendarEntry] the evaluation covers,
      which is what a page reaches and, written where nothing is scoped, is everything there is. A
      calendar asking for less would be asking in a language of its own, and a note wanting less
      than its vault already has [/Data/Note/#tasks] to ask in.
      Marked as a page of its own, so a reader can open a calendar by name without a page having
      been written to hold one.

  data(Show/Calendar(result -> String))
    # [Show/Calendar] shows what a calendar found: everything which sits on a day, each beside the
      day it sits on and the page and expression it was written as, and the day the question was
      asked on.
      Each entry is the value itself rather than a rendering of it, as [/Data/Note/Show/TaskList]
      carries its tasks and for a further reason: a calendar does not know what it is showing, so
      handing the value on as it is, is the only way to show a kind of thing it has never heard of.
      Whatever draws a value of that kind draws it here.
      The day the question was asked on travels with the answer. A reader's clock is a different
      clock in a different place, and the day a calendar marks as today has to be the day the answer
      it is drawing was worked out on.
      Which days are drawn, and whether they are drawn a day, a week or a month at a time, is not
      here. That is a reader turning over what they have already been handed rather than a second
      question, so it is decided where the calendar is drawn and nothing is asked again for it.
      One field carrying both what was found and why nothing was, as [/Data/Note/Show/TaskQuery]
      carries them and for its reason.

  every(Show/Calendar.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  /Catln/Doc/dshow(s= #calendar) =
    calendarEntries(annot) -> String
      # [calendarEntries] renders everything the evaluation covers which sits on a day as JSON: each
        the value it displays as, the day it sits on, and the page and expression it was written as.
        Which values those are is asked of the class rather than listed, so a kind of thing joins a
        calendar by saying it belongs and answering [calendarDay].
      #runtime("calendarEntries")

    Show/Calendar(result= calendarEntries(annot= s))
